<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yalu River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amnok River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yalu River forms much of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, flowing about 790 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-paektu">Mount Paektu</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a>. Its middle valley was the heartland of Goguryeo, whose capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gungnae">Gungnae</a> lay on its bank, and later Korean states treated it as their northern frontier. In October 1950 Chinese forces crossed it to enter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yalu-river.jpg" alt="Yalu River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xue siyang (df45086) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B7%A1%E9%81%93%E5%B7%A5%E5%87%BA%E5%93%81_photo_by_Xundaogong_-_panoramio_(66).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
